"Artin's conjecture on primitive roots (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artin%27s_conjecture_on_primitive_roots)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 months ago; In number theory, Artin's conjecture on primitive roots states that a given integer a that is neither a square number nor −1 is a primitive root modulo infinitely many primes p. ... Conditionally, under the Generalized Riemann Hypothesis, Gupta and Murty proved Artin's primitive root conjecture for elliptic curves with complex multiplication, for primes splitting in the relevant imaginary quadratic field.\nArtin's conjecture on primitive roots\nConjecture in number theory\n\nIn number theory, Artin's conjecture on primitive roots states that a given integer a that is neither a square number nor −1 is a primitive root modulo infinitely many primes p. The conjecture also ascribes an asymptotic density to these primes. This conjectural density equals Artin's constant or a rational multiple thereof.\n\nThe conjecture was made by Emil Artin to Helmut Hasse on September 27, 1927, according to the latter's diary. The conjecture is still unresolved as of 2026. In fact, there is no single value of a for which Artin's conjecture is proved.\n\n## Formulation\n\nLet a be an integer that is not a square number and not −1. Write a=a_0b^2 with a_0 square-free. Denote by S(a) the set of prime numbers p such that a is a primitive root modulo p. Then the conjecture states\n# S(a) has a positive asymptotic density inside the set of primes. Similarly, if a_0 is congruent to 1 mod 4, then the number needs to be multiplied by \\frac{p(p-1)}{p^2-p-1} for all prime factors p of a_0.\n\n- Gerard P. Michon. Artin's Constant. Numericana. 2006-06-15.\n\n## Examples\n\nFor example, take a = 2. The conjecture is that the set of primes p for which 2 is a primitive root has the density C_Artin. The set of such primes is \nS(2) = {3, 5, 11, 13, 19, 29, 37, 53, 59, 61, 67, 83, 101, 107, 131, 139, 149, 163, 173, 179, 181, 197, 211, 227, 269, 293, 317, 347, 349, 373, 379, 389, 419, 421, 443, 461, 467, 491, ...}. It has 38 elements smaller than 500 and there are 95 primes smaller than 500. The ratio (which conjecturally tends to C_Artin) is 38/95=2/5=0.4.\n\nFor a = 8 = 2^3, which is a power of 2, the conjectured density is \\frac{3}{5}C, and for a = 5, which is congruent to 1 mod 4, the density is \\frac{20}{19}C.\n\n## Partial results\n\nIn 1967, Christopher Hooley published a conditional proof for the conjecture, assuming certain cases of the generalized Riemann hypothesis.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA conditional proof of Artin’s conjecture for primitive roots – Mathematical Reports – Comptes rendus mathématiques (https://mathreports.ca/article/a-conditional-proof-of-artins-conjecture-for-primitive-roots/)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; A conditional proof of Artin’s conjecture for primitive roots ... Nongkynrih\n\nA conditional proof of Artin’s conjecture for primitive roots\n\nC. R. Math. Rep. Acad. Sci. Canada Vol. 23 (2) 2001, pp. 46–52\n(Received: 2000-02-10 )\n\n> A. Nongkynrih\n\nAbstract/Résumé:\n\nNo abstract available but the full text pdf may be downloaded at the title link below.\n\nKeywords:\n\nAMS Subject Classification: Congruences; primitive roots; residue systems 11A07\n\nPDF(click to download): A conditional proof of Artin's conjecture for primitive roots\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRefinements of Artin's primitive root conjecture (https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.19601)\nciteturn1academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; A famous conjecture of Artin asserts that any integer $a$ that is neither $-1$ nor a square should be a primitive root (mod $p$) for a positive proportion of primes $p$.Moreover, using a heuristic argument, Artin guessed an explicit formula for the proportion; this formula is well-supported by computations and is known to hold on a generalized Riemann hypothesis, but remains open.\nTitle: Refinements of Artin's primitive root conjecture\nAuthors: Leo Goldmakher, Greg Martin, Paul Péringuey\nDate: Wed Feb 26 22:30:15 2025\n\nA famous conjecture of Artin asserts that any integer $a$ that is neither $-1$ nor a square should be a primitive root (mod $p$) for a positive proportion of primes $p$. Moreover, using a heuristic argument, Artin guessed an explicit formula for the proportion; this formula is well-supported by computations and is known to hold on a generalized Riemann hypothesis, but remains open. In this paper we propose several conjectures that capture the finer properties of the distribution of the order of $a$ (mod $p$) as $p$ varies over primes; these assertions contain Artin's original conjecture as a special case. We prove these conjectures assuming the generalized Riemann hypothesis, as well as weaker versions unconditionally.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn generalizing Artin’s conjecture on primitive roots to composite moduli (https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/shug12.pdf)\nciteturn1search14 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; On generalizing Artin’s conjecture on primitive roots to composite moduli ... Artin conjectured that these are the only exceptional values of \\(a\\), and in fact, if \\(a\\) is not \\(-1\\) nor a square, then there is a positive proportion of primes \\(p\\) with primitive root \\(a\\). ... In a remarkable paper, Hooley [5] showed that this strong form of Artin’s conjecture follows from the Generalized Riemann Hypothesis (GRH), in particular from the Riemann Hypothesis for the Galois closures of Kummerian fields of the form \\(\\mathbb{Q}(a^{1/d})\\).\nOn generalizing Artin’s conjecture on primitive roots to composite moduli\n\nShuguang Li and Carl Pomerance\n\n1. Introduction\n\nFor a given integer \\(a\\), a natural question is whether there are infinitely many primes \\(p\\) with \\(a\\) as a primitive root; that is, the residue \\(a\\) generates the cyclic multiplicative group \\((\\mathbb{Z}/p\\mathbb{Z})^*\\). Let \\(P_a(x)\\) denote the number of primes \\(p \\le x\\) which have \\(a\\) as a primitive root. Clearly, a necessary condition on \\(a\\) for \\(P_a(x)\\) to be unbounded is that \\(a \\ne -1\\) and \\(a\\) is not a square. Artin conjectured that these are the only exceptional values of \\(a\\), and in fact, if \\(a\\) is not \\(-1\\) nor a square, then there is a positive proportion of primes \\(p\\) with primitive root \\(a\\). That is, there is a positive number \\(A(a)\\) such that\n\n\\[\nP_a(x) \\sim A(a)\\pi(x).\n\\]\n\nAfter a clarification of Heilbronn suggested by some numerical experiments of Lehmer, there is even a precise formula for the conjectured density \\(A(a)\\). In a remarkable paper, Hooley [5] showed that this strong form of Artin’s conjecture follows from the Generalized Riemann Hypothesis (GRH), in particular from the Riemann Hypothesis for the Galois closures of Kummerian fields of the form \\(\\mathbb{Q}(a^{1/d})\\).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConstants — named constants as solvency thresholds (https://antonlebed.com/growth/constants)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Named analytic constants — Golomb–Dickman, a Poisson–Dirichlet spectrum, Artin — as the solvency thresholds of designed growth economies. ... A step is transparent when the new prime adds no complexity, (p_{k}−1) | λ(p_{k−1}#); the transparent steps have density 1 (the density theorem), and the complexity has an exact ledger in the shifted primes p − 1 (the complexity ledger).\n\n# Constants\n\nNamed analytic constants — Golomb–Dickman, a Poisson–Dirichlet spectrum, Artin — as the solvency thresholds of designed growth economies.\n\nThe primorial schedule seats the primes in order: the k-th rung is p_{k}# = 2·3·5⋯p_{k}. Its unit count φ(p_{k}#) = ∏_{i≤k}(p_{i} − 1) is the rung's capacity, and its period λ(p_{k}#) = lcm(p_{i} − 1), the exponent of the unit group, its dynamical complexity. A step is transparent when the new prime adds no complexity, (p_{k}−1) | λ(p_{k−1}#); the transparent steps have density 1 (the density theorem), and the complexity has an exact ledger in the shifted primes p − 1 (the complexity ledger). What happens when a growth fate is designed to have a numerical threshold? Overlay on the schedule a reserve that earns ρ log p at each prime and pays a chosen cost w(p) ≥ 0:\n\nR(k) = ρ·θ(p_{k}) − Σ_{i≤k} w(p_{i}),  θ(x) = Σ_{p≤x} log p.\n\nThe reserve thrives when R → +∞ and sinks otherwise, and which happens is decided by a single number. The numbers that appear are the analytic-prime-constant zoo. Every in-range value below is at k = 10^{4}, the first 10^{4} primes, p ≤ 104,729.\n\n## The realizer\n\nThe realizer identity rule\n\nR(k) = θ(p_{k})·[ρ − S(k)/θ(p_{k})] with S = Σ w, so the reserve thrives iff ρ exceeds ρ_{c}(w) = lim Σ w(p_{i})/θ(p_{k}), the weight's Cesàro log-average, when it converges. Calibration weights land exactly: w = log p realizes ρ_{c} = 1.000000, sinking at ρ = 0.9 and thriving at 1.1, and w = ½ log p realizes 0.500000. Every nonnegative value is therefore trivially reachable — w = c·log p gives ρ_{c} = c — and the realizer re-expresses a prime log-average, never derives it. The content is which structured weights land on which named constants: the two families below.\n\nScope. Elementary, from the reserve's per-step drift; the calibrations are exact in range.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThis is a ``preproof'' accepted article for Canadian Mathematical Bulletin (https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/270043D7BAB4CDA2A601A061FB482AA0/S0008439526101982a.pdf/large_prime_factors_of_welldistributed_sequences.pdf)\nciteturn1search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; metic sequence h